\ No newline at end of file
+`A` and `B` bind each party's stable ID and long-term public key. `AAD` binds
+the protocol version, session ID, expiry, both fresh nonces, code-entry direction,
+both identities/public keys, the exact DER TLS certificate SHA-256 fingerprint,
+the UTF-8 host and device display names, and the ordered pending cleanup-token list. This prevents
+an intermediary from substituting identities, TLS certificates, metadata, or
+revocation instructions without invalidating confirmation. The Android app checks
+the fingerprint in `PAIRING_START` against the actual certificate presented on
+the same TLS connection before displaying Allow or storing the host pin.
+
+The exchange uses the RFC 9382 confirmation order. Android holds `cB` locally
+while it sends only `pB` in `PAIRING_SUBMIT`. The host returns `cA` in
+`PAIRING_RESULT`. Android verifies `cA`, checks the actual TLS peer certificate
+against the fingerprint bound into the PAKE context, and waits for the operator's
+explicit Allow action. Only then does it release `cB` in `PAIRING_COMMIT`. The
+host verifies `cB` before committing the registry record. The confirmation sent
+in `PAIRING_COMMIT` is the SPAKE2 `cB`, not a separately derived custom commit
+MAC; it is never persisted.
+
+## Security properties and limits
+
+- A passive observer cannot test six-digit guesses offline from a recorded SPAKE2
+  exchange. An active attacker gets one online guess opportunity per live pairing
+  session. Opening sessions is limited to five attempts per five minutes per local
+  Unix-socket caller identity (UID), and only one pairing session can be active.
+- Pairing shares, identities, cleanup instructions and TLS certificate pin are
+  cryptographically bound. Replays fail because session IDs, nonces and PAKE
+  ephemerals are fresh and sessions expire.
+- The shipped Android client does not send `PAIRING_COMMIT` before explicit
+  owner approval, and the host defers its registry write until that message
+  arrives. This is client-enforced workflow ordering, not remote proof of a human
+  action; `cB` is known to the host already (SEC-035). Old pairing revocations
+  and the new record are committed in one registry write.
+- The Android device does not persist the host, certificate pin or revocation
+  token until the host acknowledges the commit on the TLS connection whose
+  certificate fingerprint was bound into PAKE.
+- SPAKE2 does not make a six-digit code high entropy. A malicious active peer can
+  make online guesses across attempts; the in-memory rate limiter resets when the
+  daemon restarts. A deployment requiring stronger online-guess resistance should
+  use a longer human secret or persistent throttling.
+- RFC 9382 is an informational RFC, not an IETF Standards Track specification.
+  This integration should receive independent cryptographic review before being
+  relied on for high-assurance deployments.
+- A compromised Linux host, Android OS, rooted device, modified APK, dishonest
+  operator, or stolen phone is outside the protection of the pairing exchange.
+
+## Revocation cleanup
+
+The Android app can retain bearer revocation tokens when it removes a host while
+the Linux side is offline. It includes those tokens in `PAIRING_SUBMIT` during a
+later re-pair. The exact ordered token list is part of PAKE AAD; an intermediary
+cannot alter it while preserving proof validity. The host validates the PAKE and
+then applies token cleanup plus insertion of the replacement record atomically.
+Invalid or unmatched tokens do not revoke another device. Tokens are capabilities
+for revocation only; they are not authentication credentials and must never be
+logged.
+
+## Upgrade requirements
+
+Protocol v2 is intentionally wire-incompatible with v1. Pairing uses new SPAKE2
+share fields, a TLS-certificate binding, and the explicit commit exchange. Both
+Linux and Android sides must be upgraded together. Existing paired keys continue
+to work for authentication, but new pairings require v2 on both peers. There is no
+fallback to v1 pairing.
