+**Remediation (protocol v2).** The ECDH-derived short authentication string was
+replaced by SPAKE2-P256-SHA256-HKDF-HMAC, based on the construction and key
+schedule in RFC 9382. The host sends a blinded SPAKE2 initiator share; the device
+enters the host-generated six-digit password locally and sends a responder share
+plus directional confirmation. The password itself is never transmitted. The
+SPAKE2 transcript binds host and device identities/public keys, both fresh
+nonces, both PAKE shares and the fixed-width password scalar. HKDF AAD additionally
+binds the session, expiry, TLS certificate fingerprint, device name, code-entry
+mode and ordered cleanup-token list.
+
+The host returns its RFC 9382 initiator confirmation `cA`. Android checks the
+actual TLS peer certificate against the fingerprint authenticated by PAKE, then
+requires an explicit Allow before returning the responder confirmation `cB`.
+The host only persists after this separate authenticated
+`PAIRING_COMMIT`; Android persists its host key, TLS pin and revocation token only
+after `PAIRING_COMMIT_RESULT`. Pending revocations and the new registry entry are
+committed in one atomic registry save. The wire protocol version is now 2 and
+there is no v1 pairing fallback.
+
+**Regression coverage added.** `linux/protocol/src/pake.rs` has positive and
+wrong-password confirmation tests; `protocol/test-vectors/pairing.json` records
+deterministic host/device shares, confirmations and commit proof. The Kotlin
+`Spake2DeviceSession` independently checks the same fixture. Negative-vector
+coverage changes a device key or cleanup token and verifies the confirmation no
+longer matches. Daemon security tests cover persistence ordering and final commit.
+
+**Residual risk.** The code is still only six digits. SPAKE2 prevents passive
+offline guessing, but an active peer can make online guesses across attempts;
+the current per-local-UID pairing-session limiter is in-memory and resets on
+daemon restart.
+The implementation preserves RFC 9382's directional confirmation order:
+initiator `cA` first, responder `cB` second. RFC 9382 is Informational,
+not Standards Track. Independent cryptographic review and a persistent online
+attempt budget are still recommended before high-assurance deployment.
 
