+## Protocol-v2 implementation review (2026-11-23)
+
+The following findings were confirmed by inspecting the uploaded source tree.
+The remediations below are represented in the accompanying protocol-v2 patch;
+Rust/daemon tests could not be executed in the review environment and must be run
+before release.
+
+### SEC-025 — First-pair TLS certificate could be pinned from an intercepted connection
+
+**Severity:** High
+**Status:** Resolved in protocol v2 (static source review; runtime verification pending)
+**Affected:** `linux/rbad/src/tls.rs`, `linux/rbad/src/engine.rs`, Android pairing session and Wi-Fi transport
+
+Before v2, a first-time connection had no pin. A network attacker could terminate
+TLS with its own certificate and relay the old pairing exchange; the app could
+then save the attacker's certificate as the pin. The paired protocol key was a
+separate identity, but the transport pin was not authenticated by the pairing
+transcript. V2 includes SHA-256 of the host's actual DER certificate in PAKE AAD;
+Android compares it to the certificate on the active TLS connection before
+showing Allow. A mismatch fails pairing. This mitigation is source-reviewed but
+requires an end-to-end MITM regression test on the real TLS transport.
+
+### SEC-026 — Host registry was committed before device-owner consent
+
+**Severity:** High
+**Status:** Resolved in protocol v2 (static source review; runtime verification pending)
+**Affected:** `linux/rbad/src/engine.rs`, `android/app/.../AuthenticatorSession.kt`
+
+The previous flow persisted the new host-side device record after receiving a
+valid pairing submit. Android's separate Allow step occurred afterwards. A lost
+response, disconnect, or user cancellation could therefore leave the host with a
+record for a pairing the device had not accepted. V2 parks the verified PAKE
+candidate and waits for a separate `PAIRING_COMMIT` before persistence. The
+shipped Android client sends that message only after its Allow action. This is
+honest-client workflow ordering, not independently attestable human approval:
+the host can calculate the expected SPAKE2 `cB` itself (SEC-035). Android saves
+the host and pin only after receiving the commit acknowledgement, and now rejects
+that acknowledgement unless its local state is `PairingCommitting` (SEC-034).
+
+### SEC-027 — Pending unpair cleanup tokens were not authenticated with pairing
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Status:** Resolved in protocol v2 (static source review; runtime verification pending)
+**Affected:** `linux/protocol/src/pake.rs`, `android/protocol/.../Spake2.kt`, `linux/rbad/src/registry.rs`
+
+The old pairing confirmation did not bind the cleanup token list. An on-path
+intermediary could alter which old pairing token was submitted for cleanup without
+changing the PAKE confirmation. V2 includes the exact ordered cleanup-token list
+in authenticated context and processes it only after the final commit. Registry
+cleanup and replacement insertion are performed in one save operation with
+in-memory rollback if persistence fails.
+
+### SEC-028 — Registry loader silently skipped malformed records
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Status:** Resolved in patch (source change; Rust tests pending)
+**Affected:** `linux/rbad/src/registry.rs`
+
+The previous loader ignored malformed records rather than refusing to start. A
+later write could silently rewrite the registry without the skipped entries.
+The patched loader fails closed on invalid JSON, malformed public keys, ID/key
+mismatches and invalid names. An operator must repair or restore the registry
+rather than having the daemon silently discard trust records.
+
+### SEC-029 — Predictable temporary state-file path could follow a planted symlink
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Status:** Resolved in patch (source change; Rust tests pending)
+**Affected:** `linux/rbad/src/config.rs`
+
+The previous atomic writer used a predictable temporary filename and truncating
+open. If an attacker could create that path in the parent directory, the write
+could follow a symlink. The patched writer requires an owner-controlled 0700
+state directory, creates a unique temporary file with `create_new` and mode 0600,
+fsyncs the file, renames it atomically and syncs the parent directory. This is
+root-state hardening; it does not make an attacker-writable custom parent safe.
+
+### SEC-030 — Pairing rate-limit state is lost on daemon restart
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Status:** Confirmed residual risk
+**Affected:** `linux/protocol/src/ratelimit.rs`, `linux/rbad/src/engine.rs`
+
+The pairing rate limiter is in-memory and keyed by the local Unix control-socket
+caller identity (`uid:<uid>`), not the remote network peer. It limits session
+creation to five starts per five minutes per local UID; restarting the daemon
+resets that history. Each live SPAKE2 session permits one password attempt, but
+an active network attacker can try again when an authorized local caller opens a
+new session (or after a daemon restart). Persist rate-limit state or impose a
+durable global pairing-attempt budget before high-assurance deployment.
+
+### SEC-031 — PAM `sufficient` is not two-factor authentication
+
+**Severity:** High when marketed/deployed as MFA; Medium otherwise
+**Status:** Confirmed design property
+**Affected:** `packaging/arch/pam-*.sh`, PAM deployment documentation
+
+The shipped PAM examples use `sufficient` for the device module. In PAM,
+`sufficient` means a successful biometric-device check can satisfy the stack; it
+does not mean password AND device. The README explains this and notes the
+availability trade-off. Deployments requiring true two-factor authentication
+must configure both modules as `required` (and preserve a recovery path). Do not
+represent the default `sufficient` configuration as MFA.
+
+### SEC-032 — mDNS could overwrite the trusted host display name
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Status:** Resolved in patch (static source review; runtime verification pending)
+**Affected:** `android/app/.../HostLink.kt`, `android/app/.../AuthenticatorSession.kt`, `android/protocol/.../Messages.kt`, `linux/protocol/src/pairing.rs`, `linux/protocol/src/pake.rs`
+
+**Observation.** The Android link used `candidate.instanceName` from unauthenticated mDNS discovery to overwrite the name stored for an already paired host. The same label is shown in biometric approval UI. A LAN attacker able to spoof the mDNS record could therefore change the trusted display label without possessing the host key or completing a PAKE exchange.
+
+**Impact.** The authenticated key and TLS pin checks still controlled which host could send requests, but the user-facing attribution could be misleading. This weakens consent integrity and can make an authentication prompt appear to originate from another machine.
+
+**Recommendation / remediation.** Do not promote discovery metadata into trusted host metadata. V2 carries `host_name` in `PAIRING_START`, validates it, includes it in the PAKE AAD, and stores it only after the host confirmation is verified and the final commit succeeds. The Android link no longer rewrites a stored host name from mDNS; the initial code-entry UI explicitly labels the received name unverified until the host's PAKE proof succeeds. A cross-language negative vector proves that substituting the name invalidates PAKE confirmation.
+
+**Evidence.** `HostLink.tryCandidate`, `AuthenticatorSession.handlePairingStart`, `PairingStart::from_payload`, `PairingContext::aad`, Kotlin `PairingContext.aad`, `protocol/test-vectors/pairing.json`.
+
+### SEC-033 — Disconnected pairing connection could retain global pairing state
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Status:** Resolved in patch (static source review; Rust test added; runtime verification pending)
+**Affected:** `linux/rbad/src/transport/mod.rs`, `linux/rbad/src/engine.rs`
+
+**Observation.** The pairing worker ran independently of the socket read loop. If an unpaired peer disconnected while the worker awaited `PAIRING_SUBMIT` or `PAIRING_COMMIT`, the worker's oneshot sender remained stored in the engine and the global pairing connection claim stayed occupied until its timeout. Other peers could not attempt pairing during that interval.
+
+**Impact.** A LAN peer could cause a bounded pairing denial of service by opening a pairing connection and disconnecting before completing the exchange. Repeating this could interfere with legitimate pairing attempts.
+
+**Recommendation / remediation.** Tie the worker's lifetime to the accepted socket. The transport now aborts the worker when the read loop exits; dropping its `PairingConnectionGuard` clears the matching pending slots and cancels the active attempt while holding the claim lock, preventing teardown from erasing a later connection's state. A regression test checks immediate cleanup.
+
+**Evidence.** `Connection::run`, `PairingConnectionGuard::drop`, `Engine::cancel_pairing`, `dropping_pairing_connection_cancels_pending_attempt_immediately`.
+
+### SEC-034 — Pairing commit acknowledgement could bypass the Android Allow gate
+
+**Severity:** High
+**Status:** Resolved in patch (static source review; Android regression test pending)
+**Affected:** `android/app/src/main/kotlin/io/github/burtvadr/rba/session/AuthenticatorSession.kt`
+
+**Observation.** After `PAIRING_RESULT` verified `cA`, the client held a
+`pendingConfirmation` while displaying the explicit Allow screen. The old
+`handlePairingCommitResult` accepted a success response based only on the session
+ID, status and presence of a revocation token. It did not require the session to
+be in `PairingCommitting`. A peer that had completed the PAKE exchange could send
+a success-shaped `PAIRING_COMMIT_RESULT` before the operator tapped Allow, causing
+the client to persist the host key and TLS pin prematurely.
+
+**Impact.** A host could bypass the last local pairing-consent screen and cause
+itself to be stored as trusted, despite the Android user never tapping Allow.
+
+**Recommendation / remediation.** Require the exact `PairingCommitting` state
+before accepting the commit acknowledgement. Set that state before enqueueing
+`PAIRING_COMMIT` to avoid a fast-response race. On an unexpected or invalid
+acknowledgement, wipe pending PAKE material, cancel the timeout, clear the pending
+state and close the bootstrap connection. The patch implements these checks.
+
+**Evidence.** `AuthenticatorSession.confirmPairing`,
+`AuthenticatorSession.handlePairingCommitResult`,
+`AuthenticatorSession.abandonPendingConfirmation`.
+
+### SEC-035 — PAKE confirmation MAC is not proof of human approval
+
+**Severity:** Medium (High if deployments rely on the host-side commit as an
+independent consent factor)
+**Status:** Confirmed design limitation
+**Affected:** `linux/protocol/src/pake.rs`, `linux/rbad/src/engine.rs`,
+`android/protocol/src/main/kotlin/io/github/burtvadr/rba/protocol/Spake2.kt`,
+protocol-v2 pairing specification
+
+**Observation.** SPAKE2's responder confirmation `cB` is an HMAC under a key
+both parties derive from the PAKE exchange. The host derives the expected `cB`
+inside `PakeHostSession::finish` before the device sends `PAIRING_COMMIT`. Thus
+receipt of `cB` confirms the transcript value but is not an unforgeable signature
+or attestation that a human tapped Allow: the host already knows the expected
+MAC, and a modified device client can send it without showing the UI.
+
+**Impact.** The final message is useful for sequencing and for keeping the
+shipped Android client from persisting before its local Allow action. It does
+not let the host independently verify human approval and must not be treated as
+a second authentication factor or consent attestation.
+
+**Recommendation.** Keep the Android-side state gate and describe the host-side
+commit as client-enforced workflow ordering. If a host must rely on cryptographic
+evidence of an approval action, introduce a separate device-key signature over a
+fresh pairing-commit challenge, generated through a user-authentication-bound
+Keystore operation; assess Key Attestation separately if the host must verify
+hardware enforcement. That would be a distinct protocol extension, not something
+provided by SPAKE2 confirmation itself.
+
+**Evidence.** `PakeHostSession::finish` computes both directional confirmation
+values; `PakeHostFinish::verify_device_commit` checks the already-derived
+expected value. Android `confirmPairing` is the client-side place that delays
+sending `cB` until Allow.
+
