+### 6.8 Security properties and limitations
+
+A passive observer cannot test six-digit guesses offline from a recorded SPAKE2
+exchange. An active attacker can make one online guess per live session and can
+try again when an authorized local caller opens another session. Session creation
+is limited per local Unix-socket UID, but the limiter is in-memory and resets on
+daemon restart. SPAKE2 does not make a six-digit password high entropy. RBA
+uses the RFC 9382 construction and key-confirmation order, but its SHA-256
+password-to-scalar mapping is not memory-hard (RFC 9382 recommends an MHF), and
+its AAD profile is project-specific. RFC 9382 is informational (not Standards
+Track); independent cryptographic review and a persistent online attempt budget
+are still required before high-assurance use. A compromised endpoint or dishonest
+operator is outside the protection of the exchange.
 
